      "Test rework displacement between teams findings under plausible boundary changes: reopened-item treatment, missing evidence, age thresholds, outage periods, owner-wait classification, corrected records, and high-consequence strata. If conclusions change materially, show both readings and name the decision that depends on the definition. Examine alternative explanations such as case mix, intake redesign, manager availability, system migration, policy revision, training, improved detection, source quality, customer response, and informal help. This is descriptive research unless stronger design supports causation. For Downstream Rework Discoverability Between Outsourced and Internal Teams, analysis checkpoint 8 preserves its eligible denominator, missing-evidence count, adverse case, owner disposition, and the boundary between operational observation and consequential decision.",
      "Downstream teams may not link their repair to the original item, and follow-up windows can miss late consequences; the study describes recorded displacement rather than total hidden cost. Results also depend on record accuracy, observation period, workflow, consequence tier, completion actor, destination, review depth, error family, repair team, delay band, and recurrence history, reviewer judgment, and access to protected evidence. Public security, privacy, labor, digital, data-quality, and accessibility sources provide context but do not certify a provider or decide a company-specific matter. Place limitations beside findings and preserve unknown states so decision makers can distinguish evidence from assumption. A declared limitation is more useful than an invented value. For Downstream Rework Discoverability Between Outsourced and Internal Teams, analysis checkpoint 9 preserves its eligible denominator, missing-evidence count, adverse case, owner disposition, and the boundary between operational observation and consequential decision.",
